1
00:00:00,170 --> 00:00:01,300
Remember the Emu War?

2
00:00:01,620 --> 00:00:03,440
The soldiers actually came
back.

3
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:07,250
After the first
embarrassing withdrawal
in November 1932,

4
00:00:07,610 --> 00:00:10,290
farmers insisted the crop
damage was still serious.

5
00:00:10,760 --> 00:00:12,140
The small gun team
returned.

6
00:00:12,540 --> 00:00:13,350
By December,

7
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:17,080
Major Meredith reported
nine hundred and eighty
six emus killed.

8
00:00:17,560 --> 00:00:18,650
That was not zero,

9
00:00:18,980 --> 00:00:21,400
but it was nowhere near
enough to stop the
migration.

10
00:00:21,870 --> 00:00:23,490
Newspapers loved the
story.

11
00:00:23,930 --> 00:00:25,650
The farmers still needed
protection.

12
00:00:26,130 --> 00:00:27,200
In 1934,

13
00:00:27,510 --> 00:00:30,830
the government supplied
ammunition and paid
bounties for emu hunting.

14
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:34,940
More than fifty seven
thousand bounty claims
were made in six months.

15
00:00:35,350 --> 00:00:38,840
But the lasting physical
defence was not another
machine gun.

16
00:00:39,260 --> 00:00:41,840
It was longer fencing
across the farm country.

17
00:00:42,260 --> 00:00:43,820
The so-called war ended;

18
00:00:43,820 --> 00:00:47,080
the fight to protect crops
did not.

